---
import TableScroll from '../../../components/TableScroll.astro';
import PtosGuide from '../../../components/PtosGuide.astro';
import { elfEntryPath } from '../../../data/manual-structure';
---
<PtosGuide title="External desktop and console" description="Install EmuDesk and EmuCON on the boot drive used by your build." sources={[
  ['ptos', 'desk/Kconfig', 'External EmuDesk requirements and startup'],
  ['ptos', 'cli/Kconfig', 'External EmuCON requirements and startup'],
  ['ptos', 'include/shells.h', 'Shell search contract'],
  ['ptos', 'util/shells.c', 'Executable names and drive search'],
  ['ptos', 'aes/gemshlib.c', 'Disk desktop startup with kernel AES'],
  ['ptos', 'configs/pc-x86_64-desktop_defconfig', 'PC desktop build configuration'],
  ['ptos', 'lib/ldso/build.mk', 'ELF runtime installation tree'],
  ['ptos', 'lib/aes/build.mk', 'libaes installation path'],
  ['ptos', 'desk/deskmain.c', 'External EmuDesk accessory selection'],
  ['ptos', 'doc/pexecve-migration.txt', 'Native EmuCON and external shell vector launch'],
]}>
  <h2 class="prm-section-heading ruled">Boot files</h2>
  <TableScroll label="Boot-drive paths">
    <table class="prm-rule-table prm-key-table">
      <thead><tr><th scope="col">Boot-drive path</th><th scope="col">Purpose</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><th scope="row"><code>\EMUDESK.PRG</code></th><td>Desktop executable for builds with <code>CONF_EXTERNAL_DESK</code>.</td></tr>
        <tr><th scope="row"><code>\EMUDESK.INF</code></th><td>EmuDesk settings.</td></tr>
        <tr><th scope="row"><code>\EMUCON.TOS</code></th><td>Console executable for builds with <code>CONF_EXTERNAL_CLI</code>.</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </TableScroll>
  <p>Copy the desktop and console built for the same target as the kernel. ARM and x32 executables use ELF even though the
    filenames end in <code>.PRG</code> and <code>.TOS</code>. The 68k build uses the format enabled by the selected toolchain
    and loader. ROM configurations can include EmuDesk or EmuCON in the image.</p>

  <h2 class="prm-section-heading ruled">Shell lookup</h2>
  <p>For a disk desktop, pTOS looks for <code>EMUDESK.PRG</code> and then <code>DESKTOP.PRG</code>.
    The external console lookup tries <code>EMUCON.TOS</code>, <code>EMUCON2.TOS</code>, <code>COMMAND.TOS</code>
    and <code>SHELL.TOS</code> in order. The boot-drive root is searched first. When the welcome screen
    selects another boot drive, the shell search can then use the default boot drive’s root.</p>
  <p>On the x32 libaes desktop build, the BIOS starts the resident EmuDesk directly. If it returns or cannot be started,
    pTOS can fall back to external or embedded EmuCON, according to the CLI configuration. With a legacy kernel AES,
    the AES starts the disk desktop. If it has no embedded console, the legacy GUI search continues from the desktop names
    to <code>EMUCON.TOS</code>, <code>EMUCON2.TOS</code>, <code>COMMAND.TOS</code>, <code>SHELL.PRG</code> and <code>SHELL.TOS</code>.</p>

  <h2 class="prm-section-heading ruled">x32 shared runtime</h2>
  <p>For the <code>pc-x86_64-desktop</code> configuration, install the staged <code>GEMSYS</code> tree from
    <code>obj/ldso/runtime/GEMSYS</code> on the EFI boot volume alongside the desktop and console.
    Keep <code>LD.SO</code>, <code>LIBPTOS.SO</code> and <code>LIBAES.SO</code> under <code>\GEMSYS\LIB\X32</code>.
    A dynamically linked EmuCON still needs its runtime files when pTOS falls back to it.</p>
  <p>External EmuDesk starts up to six <code>.ACC</code> files from the root of its initial drive once per desktop session.
    These are native x32 ELF executables linked with the shared runtime and libaes. EmuDesk uses
    <a href="/historical/en/shel-write/#ptos-launch"><code>SWM_LAUNCHACC</code></a> to request accessory startup.
    See <a href={`${elfEntryPath}#startup-role`}>ELF program and accessory startup</a> for linking instructions.</p>
  <h2 class="prm-section-heading ruled">EmuCON program arguments</h2>
  <p>ARM and x32 EmuCON start external commands with <a href="/reference/pexecve/">Pexecve(0)</a>. The resolved path
    selects the executable. The typed command stays in <code>argv[0]</code>, including when EmuCON resolves it through
    <code>PATH</code> or adds an extension. Children inherit the current directory, explicit environment and stdout redirection.</p>
  <p>The native editor scrolls horizontally and accepts up to 199 input bytes and 30 arguments, including the command name.
    Quoted spaces and empty arguments survive the launch. The classic 68k console passes the historical GEMDOS tail.
    See <a href={elfEntryPath}>ELF32 process entry</a> for native vector startup.</p>
</PtosGuide>
